#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186321097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2d7d4db-e9e1-54a0-aadb-42447771855d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain Where All Partial Derivatives Exist"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a5e107b-3f50-5bd8-bbcd-305062ce3cdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ge 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321099} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f67e50-fb3e-5c99-b805-8da7432be84a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321100} {:answer/id #uuid "41a4562c-39f9-59ee-9f23-b100e444c6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321101} {:answer/id #uuid "f2815d82-add3-5b62-b7a6-1f96bccbae5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321102} {:answer/id #uuid "42764dc4-d6a8-54de-ab9c-6e12289fb360" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\ne 0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2815d82-add3-5b62-b7a6-1f96bccbae5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}>0}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321102} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a0278f4-7ef2-5c9f-a5d9-85a1f6bca7e8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed7bcfe5-c1ea-56d9-b1e4-351fef6e9306" :question/math-academy-id "q-72005" :question/problem "Given that $g(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂g}{∂x}=\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}=\\frac{2xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nFind the set of real values $(x,y)$ where all partial derivatives of $g$ exist. Choose the displayed set that expresses the required condition on $x^2+y^2$ explicitly as a strict positivity inequality." :question/worked-solution "For real $x$ and $y$, each square is nonnegative, so $x^2+y^2\\ge 0$. Their sum equals zero exactly when $x=y=0$. Consequently,\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2\\ne 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2>0.\n$$\n\nThe logarithm in $g(x,y)=x\\ln(x^2+y^2)$ requires $x^2+y^2>0$. This same condition makes the denominators in both supplied partial derivatives nonzero. On this open set the logarithm and rational terms are differentiable, so both partial derivatives exist. At the origin the original function is undefined, so its partial derivatives do not exist there.\n\nThus the domain where all partial derivatives exist is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2:x^2+y^2>0\\}=\\mathbb R^2\\setminus\\{(0,0)\\}.\n$$\n\nThe choices using $x^2+y^2\\ne 0$ (c) and $x^2+y^2>0$ (e) describe exactly the same domain. The question explicitly requests the strict positivity form, so choose e. Choice c is an equivalent description of the domain, but it does not express the condition as the requested positivity inequality."}]}
